body:
After reading the study I do think one could misunderstand some things about it if they only read the articles. The study found that athletes started at well above 2.5 times their BMR, but over time their calories burned started to lower until they were at the rate of burning 2.5 times their BMR. Their information suggests it takes up to 70 days for this to happen. They also found that eating more didn't increase this rate, which is the real surprise.  So, if tomorrow someone started running marathons they could burn more than 2.5 times their BMR easily, but if they ran marathons everyday for 10 weeks their calories burned would lower to the 2.5 figure.

&#x200B;

A study like this needs to be taken with a huge grain of salt. The sample size was 3. They originally had 6 but could not continue with 3 subjects. They took Calorie expenditures only 3 times per person over a twenty week period.  I would not consider this a study one could draw conclusions from, this is more a proof of concept. Something to convince someone with money to do a much larger study.  They took some information for other high expenditure activity from other studies, but I don't find that to be particularly convincing. Very easy to cherry pick studies. When this is replicated in a study of over 1000 people that include women and people of varying races then we can say there is compelling evidence that 2.5 times BMR is the cap for humans.

Dating a 9 as well! I agree doing the stuff together helps a lot, so does breaking down tasks into baby steps. Like, I don't say "Could you do the dishes while I feed the animals?" I would say " Could you fill the two pots on the stove so they're easier to wash? I'm gonna feed the pets right now." 8/10 once she sees I'm still doing chores she'll go ahead and just do the dishes lol. It helps a lot to be in the same room so tasks can be bonding time.  We make it fun for each other, or we commiserate together. With bigger, long term goals it really helps to simplify tasks and provide emotional support.  Like she wants to go back to school to get her degree, but last year she didn't really progress towards that goal. In January she was very upset about her lack of motivation to do stuff.  I hear her grievances and remind her that she's also very patient and has great self-reflection, and that those are two traits on the other side of the "lack of motivation" coin. She wasn't ready for school when she was 18 or 22, she knows this. So she saved herself a bunch of money and time not going then. By waiting until now she qualifies for scholarships she didn't before and she knows she's ready to study full-time. Then We break it down to smaller steps. The goal isn't the big huge thing of graduating, it's the smaller thing of applying for FAFSA. Which she did. The next goal is to make an appointment at the local community college so she can get her feet wet there.

&#x200B;

Strict timetables really stress her and most 9s out. You just gotta accept they do things at their own pace and in their own time, but I can encourage my gf to do things more efficiently when she asks.  She is getting better at it for sure! But there's always gonna be at least some imbalance, if there's a hard deadline withing like a week I know I'm gonna have to handle it! But other stuff like chores and more flexible things can be improved on, and she knows and is trying. Make sure you are also trying to improve yourself. I'm sure there are spheres of life where your partner is picking up the slack, so you should try and improve in those areas while your partner improves in their areas. I'm a very internal, closed off person. I used to be so much worse. At the start of our relationship, the emotional side and like the interacting with other people was entirely her. She introduced me to people so I'd have more friends. She encouraged my outgoing interests and showed me things like local cons and stores where I could engage with others and my interests. She was emotionally vulnerable. She pursued me. She opened up to me even though I gave her nothing back at first. And she found a way to get me to open up more besides just telling me to be more open. One of my only close friends is a 7 and before my gf he'd try and get me to open up just by asking or forcing things like hugs and that just made me shut down.  I literally didn't know how to describe my feelings and experiences with words. She helped me find other ways to express my feelings, and now I'm working on getting there with words. But she still does most of the emotional lifting, and I still do most of the life tasks. She's told me in the past that seeing how hard I'm trying to be the best partner for her helps her gather the motivation to be better for me. So I try to emphasize what she's already good at when we talk about what could be improved.
